================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  One arm of the controlled A/B the AAP mandates (0.5.1, 0.9.1): the pinned
  tainted-sql-string rule from the pinned Opengrep ruleset, run over Spark's
  own Scala file core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/storage/DiskStore.scala,
  with taint analysis as the SOLE difference between the two arms. It carries
  the arm's exact command, the argv delta against its sibling, the scan root
  and the pinned HEAD it ran against, the engine, rule and ruleset identities
  it ran under, its exit code, its wall time, the findings it produced with
  every line and the dataflow traces attached to them, the output's byte size
  and sha256 measured from the file on disk after the write, and its console
  output verbatim.

  An A/B proves a capability only through the CONTRAST between two invocations
  that differ in one setting. Enabled-in-config is not proof (AAP 0.1.3), so
  this file reports the contrast as it came out rather than as it was expected
  to, and it states the verdict for the pair as well as the result for this
  arm.

================================================================================
WHY THIS FILE IS UNDER logs/ AND NEVER UNDER raw/
================================================================================

  Two independent reasons, either sufficient (AAP 0.5.1, 0.6.1). First, raw/
  is RUNNER-ONLY: it receives exactly one artifact per tool that writes one
  and nothing else, ever, so an A/B SARIF landing there could overwrite the
  opengrep.sarif the Stage 3 runner writes and the dataset would then be built
  from an A/B over one file instead of a scan over the whole scope. Second,
  this is one of the run's two deliberate second appearances: Opengrep appears
  here as the taint A/B subject and again as one of the nine scanned runners;
  Joern appears as a runner and again as the capability probe's subject. Each
  second appearance writes outside raw/ and contributes NO dataset row (AAP
  0.3.2).

